title: Ship pidly as a package: licence, version, release, tap
effort: L
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T06:43:08Z
updated: 2026-09-17T06:43:08Z

Someone clones a repo that uses pidly, or wants to start one, and installs
the CLI from their package manager. One file goes into their bin; nothing
else is needed, because there is nothing else.

Packaging is convenience and discoverability, never capability. The format
stays readable with find, sed and mv, both generated documents keep saying
so, and no instruction anywhere may come to assume pidly is installed. That
is principle 1 and this ticket must not erode it. The honest fallback, which
already works and should be documented whatever else happens, is:

  curl -o ~/.local/bin/pidly <raw url> && chmod +x ~/.local/bin/pidly

Two things block every route, both found while writing this ticket:

- There is no LICENSE file. Homebrew will not take a formula without one and
  Debian will not either. Nothing about the project says how anyone may use
  it. Pick one — MIT, ISC, 0BSD and Apache-2.0 are the plausible set for a
  single shell script — and that is an owner's decision, not an
  implementation detail.
- There is no version. The script carries no VERSION and has no --version,
  so a package has nothing to name and a user has no way to say what they
  are running. A line at the top of the script and a flag that prints it.

Then releases: a tag, a tarball with a stable URL, and a checksum. No build
step, no compilation, no dependencies beyond a POSIX shell, the usual
coreutils and an optional git.

Routes, in the order they are worth doing:

- A Homebrew tap. Immediate, no gatekeeping, works on Linux as well as
  macOS, and the formula is about ten lines ending in bin.install "bin/pidly".
- The AUR. A PKGBUILD for a single script is a few lines and is what an Arch
  user expects.
- homebrew-core later, if the project earns it. Core has notability and
  maintenance requirements a new project will not meet, and being rejected
  there costs nothing if the tap already works.
- Distro-native .deb, .rpm and nixpkgs are a maintenance treadmill, each
  with its own review and cadence. Defer until someone asks.

21 already checked the name: the Homebrew formula and cask names are free
and so is Debian. PyPI and RubyGems are permanently taken by dead projects,
which matters only if a wrapper ever wants them.

The design question underneath: what a version means for a tracker on disk.
The tracker records nothing about versions and should not start — that would
be state that can disagree with the files. But check is version-sensitive in
a way the rest is not: an older pidly reading a newer tracker flags fields it
has never heard of, so any pidly older than 17 would call created and updated
undeclared, and after 28 an older one will not find .pidly/schema at all.
Decide whether check should be lenient about what it does not recognise, or
whether release notes naming format changes are enough. Everything else —
reading, claiming, closing — degrades gracefully, because it is files.

Also decide whether a man page is worth generating from the usage text.
Debian's guidelines want one; a tap and the AUR do not care.

Sequencing: a 0.x release can go out now. Do not call anything 1.0 before 28
lands, because it changes the schema file on disk.

Worth splitting into children when it is picked up: licence, version and
releases, the tap, the AUR.

Done when a clean machine can install pidly from a tap and from the AUR, run
pidly init in a fresh repo, and pidly --version reports what was installed —
and when the documents still tell an agent in an uninstalled checkout how to
do everything by hand.
